commendation": "Relayed from the manifest's record_lint. Each is a suite-total test claim on an added line of durable prose: packages/evals/docs/open-problems.md:133; packages/evals/examples/5-writer/skills/control/exemplars/r2-config-sweep.md:3, packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/analyst/skill/exemplars/r2-config-sweep.md:3 and packages/evals/tests/fixtures/writer_cases/candidates/headline-answers/exemplars/r2-config-sweep.md:3 (exemplar prose about a fictional campaign, where the figure is content, not a claim about this repo's suite); packages/evals/tests/fixtures/writer_cases/held_out/README.md:45. Nothing reads them. Accept the exemplar copies as content, and accept or relational-ise the other two. Scope: instance.", "files": ["packages/evals/docs/open-problems.md", "packages/evals/examples/5-writer/skills/control/exemplars/r2-config-sweep.md", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/analyst/skill/exemplars/r2-config-sweep.md", "packages/evals/tests/fixtures/writer_cases/candidates/headline-answers/exemplars/r2-config-sweep.md", "packages/evals/tests/fixtures/writer_cases/held_out/README.md"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "The Result.against pin moved to the grid and lost two assertions: the rung test no longer checks that the re-read keeps the result's grader readings (and, where asked, its question)", "recommendation": "Instance. Before the split, test_invoices_rung.py checked `again.question == QUESTION and again.grader_readings == result.grader_readings` after `result.against(\"v2\")`. test_3e_the_grid_crosses_two_factors... (packages/evals/tests/test_invoices_rung.py:327-329) now checks control, run_ids and the contrast arms only. The grid asks no question, so dropping the question half follows from the move. Elsewhere the default question is carried only implicitly, through the margin it carries (test_quick_results.py:695). The commit message does not mention the narrowing. Add `again.grader_readings == result.grader_readings` to the grid test; it is one clause, the claim still holds for the grid, and the drop then has nothing left to explain.", "files": ["packages/evals/tests/test_invoices_rung.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Rung 3c (guardrail.py:28) still carries 3b's concept, a margin on total, at a value 3b teaches cannot be shown, because the file must stay the pre-split eval that the frozen rung-5 case and five presentation-golden tests are pinned to", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The split's stated design is one idea a file (ladder.md:1, CHANGELOG, examples/README.md:5 'each file adds one idea to the one before'). guardrail.py is not 3a plus a guardrail. It is 3a plus grader(\"total\", margin=0.05) plus the guardrail. 3b (margin.py, ladder.md 'Rung 3b' lesson) says 20 points is the tightest margin 30 invoices can show, so 3c models the exact choice 3b just warned against. Both docs then need a parenthetical apologising for it (ladder.md:337, examples/README.md:170). A reader who copies the guardrail example copies an unshowable margin. Cause: guardrail.py doubles as the fixture behind the rung-5 frozen case (export_writer_cases.py LADDER) and the 'rung3' goldens in test_presentation_first_screen/names/figure_forms/one_reading and test_scorecard_baseline. The frozen case does not need the file while its case file exists. Fix: make guardrail.py compare.py plus the guardrail only, and move the pre-split eval into a test fixture that the export LADDER and the rung3 presentation tests load. Either regenerate those goldens or keep them on the fixture. If the coupling stays on purpose, record that decision where the ladder states the one-idea-a-file rule, and do not claim 'each file adds one idea to the one before'.", "files": ["packages/evals/examples/3-invoices/guardrail.py", "packages/evals/docs/ladder.md", "packages/evals/examples/README.md", "packages/evals/tests/fixtures/writer_cases/export_writer_cases.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Two near-identical rung-copy helpers, ladder_support.offline_copy and rung_support.rung_copy, have needed every fix twice (sys.path giveback in 7bffdeb94, sibling eviction in this delta) and have already drifted: only offline_copy ignores .evals/report/__pycache__", "recommendation": "Scope: class, bounded to packages/evals/tests/{ladder_support,rung_support,example_loader}.py. The delta added evict_siblings and had to call it from both copy functions (ladder_support.py:53, rung_support.py:45). The two still differ. rung_copy (rung_support.py:40) copies a checkout's gitignored .evals/ store, report/ and __pycache__ when a developer has run an example in place, so test_doc_blocks and the presentation tests that use it can start from a stale store that offline_copy-based tests never see. The duplication goes further: recorded_spend (ladder_support) and recorded_cost (rung_support) both total a recording's cost_usd, and EXAMPLES is defined in both ladder_support.py and example_loader.py. Construction: keep one copy helper, with the ignore patterns, live-variable list and eviction in one place; keep one recorded-cost reader and one EXAMPLES. Point every rung test at those, so the next fix lands once.", "files": ["packages/evals/tests/ladder_support.py", "packages/evals/tests/rung_support.py", "packages/evals/tests/example_loader.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "examples/README.md:45 gives the wrong reason ci.py and serve.py run from their own directory: every rung-3 file imports a sibling (invoice) and still runs from anywhere through the door", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. quick/files.py puts a file's directory on sys.path and reads its relative paths beside it. Plain `python ci.py` already puts the script's directory on sys.path. So 'because each imports a sibling as a module' is contradicted on the same page by compare.py, margin.py, guardrail.py and grid.py. The real constraint is that under plain `python`, the relative paths (cases, recording, the .evals store, report/) resolve against the working directory. The instruction itself (cd first) is right, so nothing anyone does goes wrong. Delete the 'because' clause, or state the working-directory reason.", "files": ["packages/evals/examples/README.md"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Rung 3b's margin is justified by the readings it judges: margin.py:3-4 picks 20 points because 'thirty invoices narrow v2's interval on total to 19 points either side, so a 20-point margin is the tightest they can show it inside'", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (three sites, one teaching). The package's own rule (measuring-soundly.md:163-165, 'What pre-registration guards against is criterion selection: a margin, floor, bar, control or aspect chosen once the readings it judges could be seen') says a margin chosen after the interval was seen is criterion selection. The example that teaches equivalence gives exactly that as its reason. The quick path dates the margin at Eval construction, so the page's late-criteria disclosure can never name it. ladder.md:283 then says the margin is 'decided before the readings', which contradicts the docstring next to it. Consequence: a reader learns to set a margin just wider than the observed interval to get 'equivalent', and the report shows that as a clean pre-declared equivalence. Fix: give the 20-point margin a domain reason, meaning what difference on total is too small to act on for this use, independent of the run. Then state the case-count point as a consequence, not as the reason: with 30 invoices no margin under about 19 points could be shown. Apply the same change to margin.py:1-6, docs/ladder.md:281-286 (the 3b lesson) and examples/README.md:156-160.", "files": ["packages/evals/examples/3-invoices/margin.py", "packages/evals/docs/ladder.md", "packages/evals/examples/README.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The shared-Model replay failure the split now documents ('a replay serves each recorded call once per model', ladder.md:237) surfaces as a refusal that names a different cause and prescribes re-recording (recorded_completions.py:474-480)", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The delta makes this constraint a rule readers must follow: ladder.md:237-238, examples/README.md:136-137, and test_invoices_rung.py:369 pins each file building its own Model. It also adds tests/ladder_support.evict_siblings because the constraint bit the suite itself. A reader who folds two rung-3 evals into one script or one door run sharing `model` (the obvious refactor, since every file repeats the same Model line) gets every cell of the second eval excluded as a rig fault. The message says 'the code asks it more often than it did when it was recorded' and points at re-recording (`RECORD=1`, live spend). It never says the model was already drained by another eval in the process. Model's own docstring (providers/model.py:928-931, **Recording.**) does not state the once-per-instance rule either. Fix: when the over-asked prompt was fully served earlier in this client's life, say so in the refusal, along the lines of 'this Model already served every recorded call for it; two evals sharing one Model replay it twice; build one Model per eval'. Also state the rule in Model's Recording paragraph, so the constraint lives with the API and not only in the ladder.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/testing/recorded_completions.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/providers/model.py", "packages/evals/docs/ladder.md"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions of the newest delta (d8ccb9d0b..f7c2d9e4e) for the owner; the manifest names no plan (scope null), so none is judged against a plan's Goa